WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.537
BULL stands for Blocking Unauthorized Logic Loopholes.

00:00:03.757 --> 00:00:07.438
It sits between an agent and the actions its host makes available.

00:00:07.658 --> 00:00:11.013
Information can shape a request, but it should not grant permission.

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:17.247
BULL is a Python execution-governance runtime, not a new language model or a custom operating-system kernel.

00:00:18.892 --> 00:00:21.214
The model proposes an operation and resource.

00:00:21.434 --> 00:00:26.635
Separately, trusted integration code supplies identity, provenance and capability grants.

00:00:26.855 --> 00:00:31.058
The canonicalizer normalizes the request and derives the capability itself.

00:00:31.278 --> 00:00:33.530
Policy checks grants and deployment limits.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.452
Session and security-domain checks carry behavioral history across related agents.

00:00:38.672 --> 00:00:41.261
A child does not acquire more authority than its parent.

00:00:42.892 --> 00:00:47.872
Production dispatch checks the exact executable and every argument against host authorization.

00:00:48.092 --> 00:00:51.355
A dispatcher-issued permit binds that action to the runtime call.

00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:52.701
Denied requests stop.

00:00:52.921 --> 00:00:55.092
Escalated requests return for review.

00:00:55.312 --> 00:01:01.314
Requests that continue still pass the execution checks; an allow decision does not mean unrestricted host access.

00:01:02.975 --> 00:01:07.723
Before command execution, BULL admits a bounded project tree into a private snapshot.

00:01:07.943 --> 00:01:12.576
File manifests and copy checks reject unsupported entries and detect source changes.

00:01:12.796 --> 00:01:15.257
The snapshot is scanned, then hashed again.

00:01:15.477 --> 00:01:18.368
The namespace runtime mounts this admitted copy read-only.

00:01:18.588 --> 00:01:21.200
It does not hand the agent the entire host filesystem.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:29.653
The namespace backend creates separate user, mount, process, network and interprocess-communication views.

00:01:29.873 --> 00:01:33.937
Trusted security code comes from a read-only runtime mount, separate from the project.

00:01:34.157 --> 00:01:35.956
Seccomp limits system calls.

00:01:36.176 --> 00:01:38.452
Landlock restricts filesystem access.

00:01:38.672 --> 00:01:41.830
No-new-privileges and resource limits add constraints.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:46.717
These controls use the host Linux kernel; they do not create a virtual machine.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:52.100
BULL bounds project admission, process resources and captured output.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.083
Delegated control groups add workload-wide limits.

00:01:55.303 --> 00:01:59.494
The trusted bootstrap emits a nonce-bound record before executing the command.

00:01:59.714 --> 00:02:04.207
In this source revision, the parent validates that record after the bounded child run.

00:02:04.427 --> 00:02:07.318
That is not a parent-acknowledged pre-execution launch handshake.

00:02:08.975 --> 00:02:15.175
The optional outer launcher targets Linux x eighty-six sixty-four with K V M hardware virtualization.

00:02:15.395 --> 00:02:17.566
QEMU boots a locally supplied guest kernel.

00:02:17.786 --> 00:02:22.557
Three read-only disks provide the root filesystem, admitted project and trusted runtime.

00:02:22.777 --> 00:02:26.516
Guest initialization mounts them and starts an explicit engine.

00:02:26.736 --> 00:02:28.454
There is no guest network device.

00:02:28.674 --> 00:02:33.190
Persistent sessions and real-hardware release certification remain unfinished.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:40.307
On the trusted-host integration path, secret and network brokers perform separate authorization.

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:43.313
Secret grants can limit names, lifetime and use count.

00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:50.070
Outbound requests are checked against host restrictions and pinned addresses, with TLS hostname verification for HTTPS.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:55.421
These broker components do not imply a working network channel inside the networkless MicroVM.

00:02:57.058 --> 00:02:59.276
Audit records form a local hash chain.

00:02:59.496 --> 00:03:07.193
The production HTTPS transport sends an authenticated checkpoint containing the session, sequence and head hash, rather than the full record.

00:03:07.413 --> 00:03:11.314
The service returns an acknowledgement that is checked against those exact values.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:16.712
Guest relay classes exist, but production guest activation still requires a trusted bootstrap.

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:24.712
For namespace execution, BULL returns an exit code and bounded standard-output and standard-error streams to the trusted caller.

00:03:24.932 --> 00:03:27.092
The private snapshot is then destroyed.

00:03:27.312 --> 00:03:30.319
This is not a general writable-artifact promotion system.

00:03:30.539 --> 00:03:39.095
The MicroVM launcher supervises its QEMU child and cleans temporary assets; a persistent guest result and export protocol is still release work.

00:03:40.725 --> 00:03:48.887
The repository combines policy, dispatch, snapshots, Linux enforcement, brokers, audit services and bounded formal models.

00:03:49.107 --> 00:03:53.228
Its tests support specific implemented behaviors, not universal safety.

00:03:53.448 --> 00:03:59.532
A reproducible guest build, persistent guest integration and real K V M release evidence remain unfinished.

00:03:59.752 --> 00:04:03.038
BULL has not undergone an independent third-party security audit.
